Spec: Feature transformation_errors.created_at as TIMESTAMPTZ

Purpose:
  Store `transformation_errors.created_at` with time zone, so the http-server can decode it as `DateTime<Utc>`.
  Before, `GET /api/v1/logs/transformation-errors` answered HTTP 500 as soon as the table contained rows.
  Issue: https://github.com/Zheng-Bote/mitm_transformation_main/issues/9

Platform: PostgreSQL

References:
  ../../../mitm-2.sdd
  ../../../transformation-layer/transformation-layer.sdd

Owns:
  ./**

Can modify:
  /transformation-layer/migrations/**
  /migrations/setup.sql
  /docs/Transformation-layer/README.md
  /CHANGELOG.md

Decisions:
  Existing values are interpreted as UTC (`AT TIME ZONE 'UTC'`), because the Rust transformation job writes via sqlx, which sets the session `TimeZone` to `UTC`, so `DEFAULT NOW()` stored UTC wall-clock time.
  The column is changed by a new migration instead of editing `006_transformation_errors.sql`, so existing databases are migrated and `setup.sql` stays append-only.

Must:
  `transformation_errors.created_at` is `TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW()`.
  The migration converts only a `timestamp without time zone` column and is a no-op otherwise (idempotent, safe to re-run with `setup.sql`).
  No rows are lost or reordered.

Must not:
  Change code in `mitm_transformation_main` or `core-layer/mitm_http-server`; both already work with `TIMESTAMPTZ`.

Tasks:
  [x] #1 Add `transformation-layer/migrations/009_transformation_errors_timestamptz.sql`.
  [x] #2 Append the migration to `migrations/setup.sql`.
  [x] #3 Update `CHANGELOG.md` and `docs/Transformation-layer/README.md`.
  [ ] #4 Verify on the dev database: run the migration, re-run it, call `GET /api/v1/logs/transformation-errors`.

Done when:
  `\d transformation_errors` shows `created_at timestamp with time zone not null default now()`, re-running the migration changes nothing, and `GET /api/v1/logs/transformation-errors` answers 200 with rows.

Scenario: existing database with error rows
  Given `transformation_errors.created_at` is `timestamp without time zone`
  And a row has `created_at = '2026-10-17 10:03:00'`
  When the migration runs
  Then the column type is `timestamp with time zone`
  And the row has `created_at = '2026-10-17 10:03:00+00'`

Scenario: migration re-run
  Given `transformation_errors.created_at` is already `timestamp with time zone`
  When the migration runs again
  Then nothing changes

Scenario: API with error rows
  Given `transformation_errors` contains rows
  When a client requests `GET /api/v1/logs/transformation-errors`
  Then the http-server answers 200 with the rows
